{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 30,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Due to the crazy technological challenges that had to be overcome in order to detect gravitational waves,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5290,
    "dDurationMs": 8810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some people were skeptical that scientists had actually done it, that they'd actually seen gravitational waves from black holes - after all, there was no corroboration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14139,
    "dDurationMs": 7910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, after the discovery that's being announced today, there can be no more doubt, because scientists have detected the merging of two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22420,
    "dDurationMs": 3289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutron stars a hundred and thirty million light years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25779,
    "dDurationMs": 5149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the first ever detection of gravitational waves from in-spiraling neutron stars,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30929,
    "dDurationMs": 7770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and what's really exciting about this detection is that the same event has been observed with telescopes in all areas of the electromagnetic spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39340,
    "dDurationMs": 3379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It all began on August 17th at 8:41 a.m."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43090,
    "dDurationMs": 3469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eastern Time when LIGO interferometers identified a clear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46840,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational wave signal that lasted about a hundred seconds, which is way longer than any previous detection, and it's consistent with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53829,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theoretical predictions for the signal from two merging neutron stars. [\"hear\" the sound of two stars colliding ♪]"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57760,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Around 1.7seconds later NASA's Fermi gamma-ray telescope identified a burst of gamma rays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64180,
    "dDurationMs": 4069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For decades gamma-ray bursts have been thought to come from neutron star mergers,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68250,
    "dDurationMs": 7169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the evidence has been lacking to know for sure that these gravitational waves and the gamma ray burst came from the same event."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75420,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key was to locate where in the sky this neutron star merger occurred."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79500,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike a merger of black holes neutron stars emit light when they smash together and continue emitting electromagnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86080,
    "dDurationMs": 7729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation afterwards. The Fermi gamma-ray Space Telescope identified a large patch of the sky roughly the size of six thousand full moons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94360,
    "dDurationMs": 3349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Using the European Space Agency's integral gamma-ray satellite,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97710,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they were able to narrow down that range. Now, the gravitational waves detected by LIGO allowed them to identify two long strips in the sky,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105130,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of which overlapped with the existing search area. Now, interestingly,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109149,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Virgo, which is the newest gravitational wave detector, which is in Italy - it was online at the time,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115530,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it should have easily been able to detect these gravitational waves, and yet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119430,
    "dDurationMs": 4049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it saw almost nothing, and that was kind of a key clue, because it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123680,
    "dDurationMs": 7180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "indicated that the gravitational waves must be coming from one of that detector's blind spots. Every interferometer has some blind spots"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130860,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where, if the waves are coming at that angle, it's symmetric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133840,
    "dDurationMs": 7960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with respect to the two arms and so it just can't be detected. So this helped further narrow the search area down to the size of about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141890,
    "dDurationMs": 6589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "144 full moons. Now, within that area, around fifty galaxies were identified to be studied with optical telescopes, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148720,
    "dDurationMs": 6820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just 11 hours after the initial detection astronomers located a bright spot in the galaxy NGC 4993."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You are seeing here pictures of the light from two neutron stars that merged"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161569,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "130 million years ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163879,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Watch how the color and brightness changes in the aftermath of the collision."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168799,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what are neutron stars? Well,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171459,
    "dDurationMs": 3270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're the leftover cores of big stars that have exploded -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174730,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they've gone supernova. Now, those remaining cores are squeezed down by gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179560,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if they're too big, say larger than two or three solar masses, well,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183250,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they will keep on getting crushed until they collapse in on themselves forever and become a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188080,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if those cores are a little smaller, say"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191840,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1.1 and 1.6 times the mass of our Sun, as they were in this case, well,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196970,
    "dDurationMs": 1950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then they get squeezed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198920,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still and so electrons merge with protons to form neutrons and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202940,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrinos and the neutrinos take off and the neutrons are left in a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207560,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really, really densely packed star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209920,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the only reason the neutrons don't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212359,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combine with each other is because of a quantum principle, the Pauli exclusion principle, that basically says you can't put two of these particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219220,
    "dDurationMs": 4379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right on top of each other, and that's actually the only thing holding that neutron star up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224030,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, if you have two of these neutron stars and they are orbiting each other, well,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228250,
    "dDurationMs": 5039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then they emit some of their energy as gravitational waves, and as they do that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233329,
    "dDurationMs": 6830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they lose energy, meaning they spiral in closer to each other and when they get really close, say a few hundred kilometers apart,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240470,
    "dDurationMs": 5899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gravitational waves become intense, allowing us to detect them hundreds of millions of light years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247010,
    "dDurationMs": 5419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The collision of neutron stars creates a kilonova which spews debris out into space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252889,
    "dDurationMs": 1891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is debris that glows,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254780,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allowing us to observe what's been created - and, in fact, the new observations with light telescopes have shown that heavy elements like gold,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262130,
    "dDurationMs": 2959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lead and platinum were made in this event, and that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265220,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "helps us understand where a lot of the heavy elements in our universe come from. In my view, this event really shows us that we're"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271070,
    "dDurationMs": 6779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a new age of astronomy. We can detect gravitational waves, not just from black holes, but now from neutron stars,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277850,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can use that information to locate places in the sky where that occurred and we can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283020,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "validate that with our other telescopes looking in all parts of the electromagnetic spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288060,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, now we really have more tools to understand our universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292250,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and I just can't wait for the questions that we're going to address next and all of the different things"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297260,
    "dDurationMs": 2339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're going to be able to study as the gravitational wave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300200,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observatories get better and better. It's a phenomenal time to be studying the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305520,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Outro signal."
    } ]
  } ]
}
